{%- comment -%}
  SHUDHI — Ritual Scene

  A drawn water-and-light layer registered on top of assets/Its a ritual.webp.
  Rendered by sections/sh-ritual-benefits.liquid. Behaviour lives in
  assets/sh-ritual-scene.js; the loops and states are CSS, in shudhi-home.css §26.

  Parameters
    uid      required. Pass section.id. Namespaces every gradient id.
    cutout   optional image. Drawn INSIDE the registered svg, on the bar's own
             photo coordinates, so it needs no positioning of its own.
    splash   the soap-hover burst
    motes    integer 0-24, the HTML particle count
    states   the four per-card scene highlights
    cursor   emit the torch element

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SIX THINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. REGISTRATION IS BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY MEASUREMENT.

     object-fit:cover with the default object-position:50% 50%, and
     preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice", are THE SAME ALGORITHM: scale by
     max(boxW/intrinsicW, boxH/intrinsicH), then centre on both axes.

     So an svg carrying viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" + xMidYMid slice, positioned
     with box rules identical to .sh-ritual__bg, puts one SVG user unit on one
     photo pixel at every viewport and every aspect ratio. Drift is not
     minimised here, it is impossible. Every coordinate below is a pixel in
     that photograph and can be read straight off it.

     1536x1024 is the file's DECODED size, read from its VP8 header. The <img>
     in the section used to declare 2000x1200, which is geometrically inert
     (CSS fully determines the box) but is a trap: authoring from those numbers
     lands 30% wrong on x. They are corrected in the section.

     Three things this depends on, all of which must hold:
       - object-position stays at its 50% 50% default. Nothing sets it. If a
         focal point is ever wanted, the img's object-position and this
         preserveAspectRatio alignment keyword must move together — they are
         one knob written twice.
       - the photo never carries a transform. Overscanning it to allow a
         parallax would make cover recompute against the enlarged box and shift
         it under this overlay by exactly the overscan.
       - the scene renders only on the default-asset branch. A merchant image
         of unknown proportions makes every coordinate below wrong, so the
         section gates this render on s.bg_image == blank.

  2. THE CROP CONTRACT. slice COVERS the box, so part of the photo is always
     cut away. For a box of ratio R = W/H:

         R >= 1.5  ->  visible band is y in 512 +/- 768/R, full x
         R <  1.5  ->  visible band is x in 768 +/- 512R, full y

     The worst realistic desktop case is the widest viewport with the shortest
     section: 1920 x ~660 gives R = 2.91 -> y in [248, 776].

         EVERY COORDINATE HERE SATISFIES y IN [250, 775].
         ANYTHING THAT MUST ALWAYS BE VISIBLE SATISFIES y IN [310, 715].

     This is why the photo's own sun bloom (y 60), pond ripple epicentre
     (y 778) and lotus (y 840) are not referenced by anything below: all three
     are cropped away at 1920, and the lotus is present at 1440 and gone at
     1920, which is the worst kind of bug to ship. The rings at (776, 560)
     expand from the bar's contact point instead — being NEW disturbances they
     need no pixel-exact agreement with the photo's static ripples, so the one
     place registration precision would have been hardest is designed out.

     §26 sets display:none below 990px, matching where the connectors already
     go, so the mobile x-crop (x in [631, 905] at 375x1400) never applies.

  3. TWO SVGS, ONE ON EACH SIDE OF THE SCRIM. .sh-ritual::after darkens the
     photo. Art belonging to the scene must be darkened with it; art between
     the viewer and the scene must not. So __scene sits at z1 under the scrim
     and __air at z3 above it. §26 renumbers the scrim to z2 and .sh-wrap to
     z4 to make room — without that, ::after is the last generated child of
     .sh-ritual and paints over any equal-index sibling.

  4. ONE TRANSFORM JOB PER ELEMENT.
       .sh-ritual__stage    translateY ..... the entrance lift
       [data-ritual-depth]  translate3d .... cursor parallax, written by JS
       .sh-ritual__layer    opacity ........ the staggered fade
       the leaf elements    the ambient loop
     Putting any two on one element silently drops one.

  5. NO <filter>, NO mix-blend-mode. Both cost a CPU raster per frame and
     defeat layer flattening. Every soft edge here is a multi-stop alpha ramp
     inside a gradient. This is the same rule snippets/sh-soap-plate.liquid
     states, and the cursor torch in §26 is the effect most constrained by it.

  6. IDS ARE NAMESPACED AND EVERY url(#..) LIVES HERE. Two sections on one page
     would otherwise share one set of gradient ids, and the second would paint
     with the first's fills. Nothing in the stylesheet may reference these.

  Element budget: scene ~24 nodes, air ~31, plus {{ motes }} HTML motes.
  Against snippets/sh-soap-plate.liquid's ~118 for a much smaller box.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign u = uid -%}
{%- assign mote_count = motes | default: 16 -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ---- IN-SCENE LAYER (z1, under the scrim) --------------------------------
  Depth order back to front, and the nth-child index of each .sh-ritual__depth
  is what §26's entrance stagger keys off — reordering these reorders the
  entrance.
{%- endcomment -%}
<svg class="sh-ritual__scene" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  <defs>
    {%- comment -%}
      Four stops, not two. A two-stop ramp leaves a visible edge where it meets
      the photo because the falloff is linear in alpha; the extra stops put the
      knee where the eye looks for it. This is what replaces feGaussianBlur.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-bloom">
      <stop offset="0"    stop-color="#fff3d8" stop-opacity=".20"/>
      <stop offset=".38"  stop-color="#ffeccb" stop-opacity=".11"/>
      <stop offset=".70"  stop-color="#f6e3bd" stop-opacity=".035"/>
      <stop offset="1"    stop-color="#f6e3bd" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>

    <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-bead">
      <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity=".85"/>
      <stop offset=".55" stop-color="#e6f3ee" stop-opacity=".42"/>
      <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#cfe6dc" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>

    {%- comment -%}
      The contact shadow is the only dark mark in the scene. It is what sells
      the bar as resting ON the water rather than pasted over it, and it is
      also the element that carries the entrance's "soap settles" beat — the
      bar itself is raster and cannot move, so what moves is its shadow.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-contact">
      <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#0b1206" stop-opacity=".46"/>
      <stop offset=".62" stop-color="#0b1206" stop-opacity=".18"/>
      <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#0b1206" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>

    <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-bounce">
      <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#ffe9bd" stop-opacity=".30"/>
      <stop offset=".70" stop-color="#f0d7a4" stop-opacity=".08"/>
      <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#f0d7a4" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>
  </defs>

  <g class="sh-ritual__stage">

    {%- comment -%} 1 — caustic bloom. y 290-710, soft-edged so the band edge
        never resolves into a boundary. The atmosphere layer: lowest depth,
        because atmosphere does not slide. {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.12">
      {%- comment -%}
        --e is this layer's entrance delay, and it is set HERE rather than
        derived from :nth-child in §26. The air layer below renders its groups
        conditionally on the splash and states settings, so a positional
        selector would re-time the whole sequence the moment a merchant
        switched one of them off. An explicit value cannot drift.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-ritual__layer" style="--e:0s">
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__bloom" cx="776" cy="500" rx="420" ry="210"
                 fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-bloom)"/>
      </g>
    </g>

    {%- comment -%} 2 — the splash arc's inner rim. Traces the water already in
        the photograph, clipped to y 310-690 so nothing reaches the crop edge.
        A highlight, not an outline: it brightens what is there rather than
        drawing a second arc beside it. {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.35">
      <g class="sh-ritual__layer" style="--e:.12s">
        <path class="sh-ritual__arc"
              d="M470,330 C452,430 470,548 546,626 C620,700 700,690 776,690
                 C852,690 932,700 1006,626 C1082,548 1100,430 1082,330"
              fill="none" stroke="#dff0e8" stroke-opacity=".22" stroke-width="2.5"
              stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <path class="sh-ritual__arc sh-ritual__arc--in" style="--d:-8s"
              d="M524,360 C510,442 528,536 588,598 C648,658 712,650 776,650
                 C840,650 904,658 964,598 C1024,536 1042,442 1028,360"
              fill="none" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-opacity=".13" stroke-width="1.6"
              stroke-linecap="round"/>
      </g>
    </g>

    {%- comment -%} 3 — contact shadow, bounce light and the ambient rings.
        .sh-ritual__rise is the entrance's soap beat: 12px of travel over 900ms
        under .is-armed, which reads as the bar arriving and coming to rest.
        Rings span y 505-615, comfortably inside the core band. {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.5">
      <g class="sh-ritual__layer" style="--e:.38s">
        <g class="sh-ritual__rise">
          <ellipse cx="778" cy="548" rx="152" ry="27" fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-contact)"/>
          <ellipse cx="778" cy="527" rx="118" ry="17" fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-bounce)"/>
          {%- if cutout != blank -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              The optional cut-out, and it goes INSIDE the registered svg rather
              than beside it as an absolutely positioned <img>. That is the whole
              trick: x/y/width/height here are the photographed bar's own pixels
              (599,225 to 952,532), so the cut-out inherits the same crop law as
              everything else in this file and lands exactly on the bar it is
              replacing, at every viewport, with no positioning maths.

              It sits inside .sh-ritual__rise so it settles together with the
              contact shadow underneath it — a bar arriving apart from its own
              shadow is the one thing that would break the illusion.

              meet, not slice: a cut-out whose proportions differ from the bar's
              1.15:1 should letterbox inside that rect rather than be cropped by
              it. Sizing is therefore forgiving; POSITION is not, which is why
              the info text on this setting says to match the built-in framing.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <image class="sh-ritual__cutout"
                   href="{{ cutout | image_url: width: 800 }}"
                   x="599" y="225" width="353" height="307"
                   preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"/>
          {%- endif -%}
        </g>
        {%- comment -%} Three rings, one keyframe, three negative delays. The
            delays are what make them a sequence rather than a chord.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__ring-amb" style="--d:0s"
                 cx="776" cy="560" rx="62"  ry="21" fill="none"
                 stroke="#cfe6dc" stroke-opacity=".26" stroke-width="1.4"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__ring-amb" style="--d:-6.3s"
                 cx="776" cy="560" rx="112" ry="38" fill="none"
                 stroke="#cfe6dc" stroke-opacity=".18" stroke-width="1.2"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__ring-amb" style="--d:-12.6s"
                 cx="776" cy="560" rx="164" ry="55" fill="none"
                 stroke="#cfe6dc" stroke-opacity=".12" stroke-width="1"/>
      </g>
    </g>

    {%- comment -%} 4 — suspended beads. The photograph's droplets hang in the
        air around the splash rather than falling, so these bob and pulse
        instead of dropping. Seeded from the index with prime multipliers, the
        same deterministic scatter snippets/sh-living-conversation.liquid uses
        for its dust — stable across renders, so a merchant editing copy never
        sees the water rearrange itself. {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.7">
      <g class="sh-ritual__layer" style="--e:.26s">
        {%- for i in (1..9) -%}
          {%- assign bx = i | times: 137 | modulo: 620 | plus: 462 -%}
          {%- assign by = i | times: 211 | modulo: 330 | plus: 336 -%}
          {%- assign br = i | times: 53  | modulo: 3   | plus: 2 -%}
          {%- assign bd = i | times: 71  | modulo: 13 -%}
          {%- assign bo = i | times: 31  | modulo: 10 | plus: 10 -%}
          <circle class="sh-ritual__bead" cx="{{ bx }}" cy="{{ by }}" r="{{ br }}"
                  fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-bead)"
                  style="--d:-{{ bd }}s; --o:.{{ bo }}" opacity=".{{ bo }}"/>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </g>
    </g>

  </g>
</svg>

{%- comment -%}
  ---- VIEWER-SIDE LAYER (z3, above the scrim) -----------------------------
  Everything here reads at full strength because it is between the viewer and
  the scene: the nearest beads, the four state glows, and the splash.
{%- endcomment -%}
<svg class="sh-ritual__air" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice"
     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
  <defs>
    <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-drop">
      <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity=".92"/>
      <stop offset=".5"  stop-color="#eaf6f0" stop-opacity=".5"/>
      <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#d6ece1" stop-opacity="0"/>
    </radialGradient>

    {%- if states -%}
      {%- comment -%} One gradient per ritual state. Tints are chosen from what
          the photograph already contains at that spot — violet-ivory for the
          light above the bar, cool green for the waterline, leaf green for the
          herb side, warm amber for the sunlit side. None of them is a hue the
          brand palette does not already imply, and none is saturated enough to
          read as neon. {%- endcomment -%}
      <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-aura">
        <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#e8dcff" stop-opacity=".10"/>
        <stop offset=".45" stop-color="#efe6f6" stop-opacity=".05"/>
        <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#efe6f6" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>
      <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-cleanse">
        <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#cfe6dc" stop-opacity=".12"/>
        <stop offset=".45" stop-color="#dcefe7" stop-opacity=".055"/>
        <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#dcefe7" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>
      <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-ayurveda">
        <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#c8d9a4" stop-opacity=".11"/>
        <stop offset=".45" stop-color="#d8e5bd" stop-opacity=".05"/>
        <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#d8e5bd" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>
      <radialGradient id="sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-intention">
        <stop offset="0"   stop-color="#f2d9a8" stop-opacity=".11"/>
        <stop offset=".45" stop-color="#f6e6c6" stop-opacity=".05"/>
        <stop offset="1"   stop-color="#f6e6c6" stop-opacity="0"/>
      </radialGradient>
    {%- endif -%}
  </defs>

  <g class="sh-ritual__stage">

    {%- if states -%}
      {%- comment -%} The four glows. All rest at opacity 0 and are raised by
          §26's :has() rules — no attribute is ever written to the DOM, so
          there is no state to go stale across a shopify:section:load. All four
          sit inside the core crop band. {%- endcomment -%}
      <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.5">
        {%- comment -%} cy 405 / ry 145 spans y 260-550, not the y 210-590 a
            taller halo would want. The bar runs 225-532 and the crop contract
            cuts everything above 250, so a glow centred on the bar's TOP edge
            would have its brightest part sliced off on a wide monitor and read
            as a band across the middle instead of a halo. Crowning the
            VISIBLE part of the bar is the version that survives the crop.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__glow sh-ritual__glow--aura"      cx="776" cy="405" rx="300" ry="145" fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-aura)"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__glow sh-ritual__glow--cleanse"   cx="776" cy="560" rx="340" ry="120" fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-cleanse)"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__glow sh-ritual__glow--ayurveda"  cx="620" cy="470" rx="250" ry="200" fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-ayurveda)"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__glow sh-ritual__glow--intention" cx="900" cy="500" rx="250" ry="200" fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-glow-intention)"/>
      </g>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} Nearest beads — fewer, larger, brighter, faster. {%- endcomment -%}
    <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.85">
      <g class="sh-ritual__layer" style="--e:.3s">
        {%- for i in (1..6) -%}
          {%- assign ax = i | times: 181 | modulo: 500 | plus: 520 -%}
          {%- assign ay = i | times: 97  | modulo: 270 | plus: 364 -%}
          {%- assign ar = i | times: 41  | modulo: 3   | plus: 3 -%}
          {%- assign ad = i | times: 59  | modulo: 11 -%}
          {%- assign ao = i | times: 43  | modulo: 8   | plus: 14 -%}
          <circle class="sh-ritual__bead sh-ritual__bead--near" cx="{{ ax }}" cy="{{ ay }}" r="{{ ar }}"
                  fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-drop)"
                  style="--d:-{{ ad }}s; --o:.{{ ao }}" opacity=".{{ ao }}"/>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </g>
    </g>

    {%- if splash -%}
      <g class="sh-ritual__depth" data-ritual-depth="0.5">

        {%- comment -%} Three expanding rings. transform-box and transform-origin
            are set together in §26 and must stay that way: without the first, a
            length origin on an SVG child resolves against a box that differs by
            engine version, and every ring would scale from the section's
            top-left corner instead of the bar's contact point. {%- endcomment -%}
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__ring" style="--d:0s"    cx="776" cy="560" rx="210" ry="72" fill="none" stroke="#e8f5ee" stroke-opacity=".5" stroke-width="1.6"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__ring" style="--d:.18s"  cx="776" cy="560" rx="270" ry="92" fill="none" stroke="#e8f5ee" stroke-opacity=".34" stroke-width="1.3"/>
        <ellipse class="sh-ritual__ring" style="--d:.36s"  cx="776" cy="560" rx="330" ry="112" fill="none" stroke="#e8f5ee" stroke-opacity=".2" stroke-width="1.1"/>

        {%- comment -%} Sixteen sparks in polar coordinates with NO trigonometry.
            A static rotate() on the wrapper places the spark; one shared
            keyframe translates the child along -Y, so "up" becomes radially
            outward. The wrapper holds the static transform and the circle holds
            the animated one, which is the one-job-per-element rule applied to
            the smallest thing in the file.

            137 is the golden angle, so 16 samples spread evenly and never
            repeat. Restricting them to +/-110deg matches the photograph's water
            arc being open at the top. Throw is 60-99 units, so every spark
            lands in y 431-629 — inside the core band. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- for i in (1..16) -%}
          {%- assign sa = i | times: 137 | modulo: 220 | minus: 110 -%}
          {%- assign sr = i | times: 53  | modulo: 40  | plus: 60 -%}
          {%- assign sd = i | times: 47  | modulo: 340 -%}
          {%- assign ss = i | times: 29  | modulo: 5   | plus: 3 -%}
          <g transform="rotate({{ sa }} 776 530)">
            <circle class="sh-ritual__spark" cx="776" cy="530" r="{{ ss }}"
                    fill="url(#sh-ritual-{{ u }}-drop)"
                    style="--r:{{ sr }}px; --d:{{ sd }}ms"/>
          </g>
        {%- endfor -%}

        {%- comment -%} Rim flash along the bar's LOWER front edge. Not the top:
            the bar spans y 225-532 and y 225-250 is outside the crop band, so a
            top-edge highlight would be invisible on a wide monitor. {%- endcomment -%}
        <path class="sh-ritual__rim" d="M612,524 C660,540 892,540 940,524"
              fill="none" stroke="#fff6de" stroke-opacity="0" stroke-width="2.2"
              stroke-linecap="round"/>

        {%- comment -%} The hit target, at the bar's exact photo coordinates.
            Because it lives in the registered svg it tracks the bar at every
            viewport for free — no rect maths, no resize handler. The svg is
            pointer-events:none in §26 and this rect is the single exception.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <rect class="sh-ritual__hit" x="599" y="225" width="353" height="307" fill="transparent"/>
      </g>
    {%- endif -%}

  </g>
</svg>

{%- if mote_count > 0 -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    ---- MOTES (z3) --------------------------------------------------------
    HTML, deliberately, not more <circle>s in the svg above. A continuously
    transforming node inside an svg repaints THAT WHOLE SVG every frame, and
    these two svgs are full-bleed. As HTML each mote is auto-promoted to its own
    compositor layer and 16 of them cost effectively nothing.

    Positioned in viewport percentages rather than photo coordinates, and
    correctly so: these are air between the viewer and the scene, not part of
    it. y is held to 22-79% so a mote never drifts under the heading or a card.
    Each carries --o twice over, on the element and in the keyframe, so the
    pulse is proportional — a mote authored at .12 must not brighten to the same
    value as one at .23, or the scatter flattens into a row of identical dots.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="sh-ritual__motes" aria-hidden="true">
    {%- for i in (1..mote_count) -%}
      {%- assign mx = i | times: 137 | modulo: 92 | plus: 4 -%}
      {%- assign my = i | times: 53  | modulo: 58 | plus: 22 -%}
      {%- assign md = i | times: 71  | modulo: 23 -%}
      {%- assign mo = i | times: 31  | modulo: 12 | plus: 12 -%}
      {%- assign ms = i | modulo: 3 | plus: 4 -%}
      <i class="sh-ritual__mote"
         style="--x:{{ mx }}%; --y:{{ my }}%; --s:{{ ms }}px; --d:-{{ md }}s; --o:.{{ mo }}"></i>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if cursor -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    ---- TORCH (z3) --------------------------------------------------------
    A FIXED-SIZE element with a FIXED gradient that is only ever translated.

    The obvious implementation writes the pointer position into
    radial-gradient(.. at var(--x) var(--y) ..) on a full-section element. That
    repaints roughly a million pixels every frame — the per-frame raster cost
    this file's rule 5 exists to forbid, arriving through a door rule 5 does not
    name. This way the gradient is painted once and the layer is moved on the
    compositor. .sh-ritual is already overflow:hidden, so it clips for free.

    Honest limitation: without mix-blend-mode:screen this is a warm haze rather
    than additive light. §26 holds its peak alpha at .17 for that reason.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="sh-ritual__torch" aria-hidden="true"></div>
{%- endif -%}
